# Security and Data Access

Security is enforced at the backend/data layer, not only in the Android UI.

## Authentication

Use the selected authentication provider.

Never implement custom password storage.

Never log:

- passwords,
- authentication tokens,
- refresh tokens,
- secrets.

## Authorization

A user must only access groups they belong to.

Membership must be validated server-side.

Do not rely on:

```text
if (userIsMember) { ... }
```

in Android as the only security mechanism.

The backend/database authorization policy is authoritative.

## Group access

For every group-scoped operation verify membership/authorization server-side:

- Read group.
- Read members.
- Read expenses.
- Create expense.
- Update expense.
- Delete expense.
- Join group.
- Manage group.

## Ownership

The group OWNER may have additional permissions.

Do not assume the client can enforce ownership safely.

## Invitation codes

Invitation codes should be:

- sufficiently unpredictable,
- validated server-side,
- scoped to the group,
- resistant to trivial enumeration where practical.

Do not expose internal database identifiers as invitation secrets.

## Secrets

Never commit:

- API private keys.
- Service-role keys.
- passwords.
- signing keys.
- production credentials.

Public client configuration is not automatically a secret, but privileged backend credentials must never be shipped in the Android app.

## User data

Only store data required by the product.

Do not collect unnecessary personal information.

Avoid logging personal or financial data unless required for diagnostics, and sanitize logs accordingly.
